ce: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\nHint: Use more detailed query to get the full content of some if needed.\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"DataFrame.plot\", \"subplots\", \"DataFrame.plot subplots\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `pandas/plotting/_core.py:PlotAccessor.pie`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1531 | def pie(self, **kwargs):\n1532 |         \"\"\"\n1533 |         Generate a pie plot.\n1534 | \n1535 |         A pie plot is a proportional representation of the numerical data in a\n1536 |         column. This function wraps :meth:`matplotlib.pyplot.pie` for the\n1537 |         specified column. If no column reference is passed and\n1538 |         ``subplots=True`` a pie plot is drawn for each numerical column\n1539 |         independently.\n1540 | \n1541 |         Parameters\n1542 |         ----------\n1543 |         y : int or label, optional\n1544 |             Label or position of the column to plot.\n1545 |             If not provided, ``subplots=True`` argument must be passed.\n1546 |         **kwargs\n1547 |             Keyword arguments to pass on to :meth:`DataFrame.plot`.\n1548 | \n1549 |         Returns\n1550 |         -------\n1551 |         matplotlib.axes.Axes or np.ndarray of them\n1552 |             A NumPy array is returned when `subplots` is True.\n1553 | \n1554 |         See Also\n1555 |         --------\n1556 |         Series.plot.pie : Generate a pie plot for a Series.\n1557 |         DataFrame.plot : Make plots of a DataFrame.\n1558 | \n1559 |         Examples\n1560 |         --------\n1561 |         In the example below we have a DataFrame with the information about\n1562 |         planet's mass and radius. We pass the 'mass' column to the\n1563 |         pie function to get a pie plot.\n1564 | \n1565 |         .. plot::\n1566 |             :context: close-figs\n1567 | \n1568 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({'mass': [0.330, 4.87 , 5.97],\n1569 |             ...                    'radius': [2439.7, 6051.8, 6378.1]},\n1570 |             ...                   index=['Mercury', 'Venus', 'Earth'])\n1571 |             >>> plot = df.plot.pie(y='mass', figsize=(5, 5))\n1572 | \n1573 |         .. plot::\n1574 |             :context: close-figs\n1575 | \n1576 |             >>> plot = df.plot.pie(subplots=True, figsize=(11, 6))\n1577 |         \"\"\"\n1578 |         if (\n1579 |             isinstance(self._parent, ABCDataFrame)\n1580 |             and kwargs.get(\"y\", None) is None\n1581 |             and not kwargs.get(\"subplots\", False)\n1582 |         ):\n1583 |             raise ValueError(\"pie requires either y column or 'subplots=True'\")\n1584 |         return self(kind=\"pie\", **kwargs)\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"subplots\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/tools.py:create_subplots`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n132 | def create_subplots(\n133 |     naxes: int,\n134 |     sharex: bool = False,\n135 |     sharey: bool = False,\n136 |     squeeze: bool = True,\n137 |     subplot_kw=None,\n138 |     ax=None,\n139 |     layout=None,\n140 |     layout_type: str = \"box\",\n141 |     **fig_kw,\n142 | ):\n143 |     \"\"\"\n144 |     Create a figure with a set of subplots already made.\n145 | \n146 |     This utility wrapper makes it convenient to create common layouts of\n147 |     subplots, including the enclosing figure object, in a single call.\n148 | \n149 |     Parameters\n150 |     ----------\n151 |     naxes : int\n152 |       Number of required axes. Exceeded axes are set invisible. Default is\n153 |       nrows * ncols.\n154 | \n155 |     sharex : bool\n156 |       If True, the X axis will be shared amongst all subplots.\n157 | \n158 |     sharey : bool\n159 |       If True, the Y axis will be shared amongst all subplots.\n160 | \n161 |     squeeze : bool\n162 | \n163 |       If True, extra dimensions are squeezed out from the returned axis object:\n164 |         - if only one subplot is constructed (nrows=ncols=1), the resulting\n165 |         single Axis object is returned as a scalar.\n166 |         - for Nx1 or 1xN subplots, the returned object is a 1-d numpy object\n167 |         array of Axis objects are returned as numpy 1-d arrays.\n168 |         - for NxM subplots with N>1 and M>1 are returned as a 2d array.\n169 | \n170 |       If False, no squeezing is done: the returned axis object is always\n171 |       a 2-d array containing Axis instances, even if it ends up being 1x1.\n172 | \n173 |     subplot_kw : dict\n174 |       Dict with keywords passed to the add_subplot() call used to create each\n175 |       subplots.\n176 | \n177 |     ax : Matplotlib axis object, optional\n178 | \n179 |     layout : tuple\n180 |       Number of rows and columns of the subplot grid.\n181 |       If not specified, calculated from naxes and layout_type\n182 | \n183 |     layout_type : {'box', 'horizontal', 'vertical'}, default 'box'\n184 |       Specify how to layout the subplot grid.\n185 | \n186 |     fig_kw : Other keyword arguments to be passed to the figure() call.\n187 |         Note that all keywords not recognized above will be\n188 |         automatically included here.\n189 | \n190 |     Returns\n191 |     -------\n192 |     fig, ax : tuple\n193 |       - fig is the Matplotlib Figure object\n194 |       - ax can be either a single axis object or an array of axis objects if\n195 |       more than one subplot was created.  The dimensions of the resulting array\n196 |       can be controlled with the squeeze keyword, see above.\n197 | \n198 |     Examples\n199 |     --------\n200 |     x = np.linspace(0, 2*np.pi, 400)\n201 |     y = np.sin(x**2)\n202 | \n203 |     # Just a figure and one subplot\n204 |     f, ax = plt.subplots()\n205 |     ax.plot(x, y)\n206 |     ax.set_title('Simple plot')\n207 | \n208 |     # Two subplots, unpack the output array immediately\n209 |     f, (ax1, ax2) = plt.subplots(1, 2, sharey=True)\n210 |     ax1.plot(x, y)\n211 |     ax1.set_title('Sharing Y axis')\n212 |     ax2.scatter(x, y)\n213 | \n214 |     # Four polar axes\n215 |     plt.subplots(2, 2, subplot_kw=dict(polar=True))\n216 |     \"\"\"\n217 |     import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n218 | \n219 |     if subplot_kw is None:\n220 |         subplot_kw = {}\n221 | \n222 |     if ax is None:\n223 |         fig = plt.figure(**fig_kw)\n224 |     else:\n225 |         if is_list_like(ax):\n226 |             if squeeze:\n227 |                 ax = flatten_axes(ax)\n228 |             if layout is not None:\n229 |                 warnings.warn(\n230 |                     \"When passing multiple axes, layout keyword is ignored.\",\n231 |                     UserWarning,\n232 |                 )\n233 |             if sharex or sharey:\n234 |                 warnings.warn(\n235 |                     \"When passing multiple axes, sharex and sharey \"\n236 |                     \"are ignored. These settings must be specified when creating axes.\",\n237 |                     UserWarning,\n238 |                     stacklevel=find_stack_level(),\n239 |                 )\n240 |             if ax.size == naxes:\n241 |                 fig = ax.flat[0].get_figure()\n242 |                 return fig, ax\n243 |             else:\n244 |                 raise ValueError(\n245 |                     f\"The number of passed axes must be {naxes}, the \"\n246 |                     \"same as the output plot\"\n247 |                 )\n248 | \n249 |         fig = ax.get_figure()\n250 |         # if ax is passed and a number of subplots is 1, return ax as it is\n251 |         if naxes == 1:\n252 |             if squeeze:\n253 |                 return fig, ax\n254 |             else:\n255 |                 return fig, flatten_axes(ax)\n256 |         else:\n257 |             warnings.warn(\n258 |                 \"To output multiple subplots, the figure containing \"\n259 |                 \"the passed axes is being cleared.\",\n260 |                 UserWarning,\n261 |                 stacklevel=find_stack_level(),\n262 |             )\n263 |             fig.clear()\n264 | \n265 |     nrows, ncols = _get_layout(naxes, layout=layout, layout_type=layout_type)\n266 |     nplots = nrows * ncols\n267 | \n268 |     # Create empty object array to hold all axes.  It's easiest to make it 1-d\n269 |     # so we can just append subplots upon creation, and then\n270 |     axarr = np.empty(nplots, dtype=object)\n271 | \n272 |     # Create first subplot separately, so we can share it if requested\n273 |     ax0 = fig.add_subplot(nrows, ncols, 1, **subplot_kw)\n274 | \n275 |     if sharex:\n276 |         subplot_kw[\"sharex\"] = ax0\n277 |     if sharey:\n278 |         subplot_kw[\"sharey\"] = ax0\n279 |     axarr[0] = ax0\n280 | \n281 |     # Note off-by-one counting because add_subplot uses the MATLAB 1-based\n282 |     # convention.\n283 |     for i in range(1, nplots):\n284 |         kwds = subplot_kw.copy()\n285 |         # Set sharex and sharey to None for blank/dummy axes, these can\n286 |         # interfere with proper axis limits on the visible axes if\n287 |         # they share axes e.g. issue #7528\n288 |         if i >= naxes:\n289 |             kwds[\"sharex\"] = None\n290 |             kwds[\"sharey\"] = None\n291 |         ax = fig.add_subplot(nrows, ncols, i + 1, **kwds)\n292 |         axarr[i] = ax\n293 | \n294 |     if naxes != nplots:\n295 |         for ax in axarr[naxes:]:\n296 |             ax.set_visible(False)\n297 | \n298 |     handle_shared_axes(axarr, nplots, naxes, nrows, ncols, sharex, sharey)\n299 | \n300 |     if squeeze:\n301 |         # Reshape the array to have the final desired dimension (nrow,ncol),\n302 |         # though discarding unneeded dimensions that equal 1.  If we only have\n303 |         # one subplot, just return it instead of a 1-element array.\n304 |         if nplots == 1:\n305 |             axes = axarr[0]\n306 |         else:\n307 |             axes = axarr.reshape(nrows, ncols).squeeze()\n308 |     else:\n309 |         # returned axis array will be always 2-d, even if nrows=ncols=1\n310 |         axes = axarr.reshape(nrows, ncols)\n311 | \n312 |     return fig, axes\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:MPLPlot._setup_subplots`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n372 | def _setup_subplots(self):\n373 |         if self.subplots:\n374 |             fig, axes = create_subplots(\n375 |                 naxes=self.nseries,\n376 |                 sharex=self.sharex,\n377 |                 sharey=self.sharey,\n378 |                 figsize=self.figsize,\n379 |                 ax=self.ax,\n380 |                 layout=self.layout,\n381 |                 layout_type=self._layout_type,\n382 |             )\n383 |         else:\n384 |             if self.ax is None:\n385 |                 fig = self.plt.figure(figsize=self.figsize)\n386 |                 axes = fig.add_subplot(111)\n387 |             else:\n388 |                 fig = self.ax.get_figure()\n389 |                 if self.figsize is not None:\n390 |                     fig.set_size_inches(self.figsize)\n391 |                 axes = self.ax\n392 | \n393 |         axes = flatten_axes(axes)\n394 | \n395 |         valid_log = {False, True, \"sym\", None}\n396 |         input_log = {self.logx, self.logy, self.loglog}\n397 |         if input_log - valid_log:\n398 |             invalid_log = next(iter(input_log - valid_log))\n399 |             raise ValueError(\n400 |                 f\"Boolean, None and 'sym' are valid options, '{invalid_log}' is given.\"\n401 |             )\n402 | \n403 |         if self.logx is True or self.loglog is True:\n404 |             [a.set_xscale(\"log\") for a in axes]\n405 |         elif self.logx == \"sym\" or self.loglog == \"sym\":\n406 |             [a.set_xscale(\"symlog\") for a in axes]\n407 | \n408 |         if self.logy is True or self.loglog is True:\n409 |             [a.set_yscale(\"log\") for a in axes]\n410 |         elif self.logy == \"sym\" or self.loglog == \"sym\":\n411 |             [a.set_yscale(\"symlog\") for a in axes]\n412 | \n413 |         self.fig = fig\n414 |         self.axes = axes\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:MPLPlot._adorn_subplots`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n544 | def _adorn_subplots(self):\n545 |         \"\"\"Common post process unrelated to data\"\"\"\n546 |         if len(self.axes) > 0:\n547 |             all_axes = self._get_subplots()\n548 |             nrows, ncols = self._get_axes_layout()\n549 |             handle_shared_axes(\n550 |                 axarr=all_axes,\n551 |                 nplots=len(all_axes),\n552 |                 naxes=nrows * ncols,\n553 |                 nrows=nrows,\n554 |                 ncols=ncols,\n555 |                 sharex=self.sharex,\n556 |                 sharey=self.sharey,\n557 |             )\n558 | \n559 |         for ax in self.axes:\n560 |             if self.yticks is not None:\n561 |                 ax.set_yticks(self.yticks)\n562 | \n563 |             if self.xticks is not None:\n564 |                 ax.set_xticks(self.xticks)\n565 | \n566 |             if self.ylim is not None:\n567 |                 ax.set_ylim(self.ylim)\n568 | \n569 |             if self.xlim is not None:\n570 |                 ax.set_xlim(self.xlim)\n571 | \n572 |             # GH9093, currently Pandas does not show ylabel, so if users provide\n573 |             # ylabel will set it as ylabel in the plot.\n574 |             if self.ylabel is not None:\n575 |                 ax.set_ylabel(pprint_thing(self.ylabel))\n576 | \n577 |             ax.grid(self.grid)\n578 | \n579 |         if self.title:\n580 |             if self.subplots:\n581 |                 if is_list_like(self.title):\n582 |                     if len(self.title) != self.nseries:\n583 |                         raise ValueError(\n584 |                             \"The length of `title` must equal the number \"\n585 |                             \"of columns if using `title` of type `list` \"\n586 |                             \"and `subplots=True`.\\n\"\n587 |                             f\"length of title = {len(self.title)}\\n\"\n588 |                             f\"number of columns = {self.nseries}\"\n589 |                         )\n590 | \n591 |                     for (ax, title) in zip(self.axes, self.title):\n592 |                         ax.set_title(title)\n593 |                 else:\n594 |                     self.fig.suptitle(self.title)\n595 |             else:\n596 |                 if is_list_like(self.title):\n597 |                     msg = (\n598 |                         \"Using `title` of type `list` is not supported \"\n599 |                         \"unless `subplots=True` is passed\"\n600 |                     )\n601 |                     raise ValueError(msg)\n602 |                 self.axes[0].set_title(self.title)\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/timeseries.py:format_dateaxis`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n292 | def format_dateaxis(subplot, freq, index):\n293 |     \"\"\"\n294 |     Pretty-formats the date axis (x-axis).\n295 | \n296 |     Major and minor ticks are automatically set for the frequency of the\n297 |     current underlying series.  As the dynamic mode is activated by\n298 |     default, changing the limits of the x axis will intelligently change\n299 |     the positions of the ticks.\n300 |     \"\"\"\n301 |     from matplotlib import pylab\n302 | \n303 |     # handle index specific formatting\n304 |     # Note: DatetimeIndex does not use this\n305 |     # interface. DatetimeIndex uses matplotlib.date directly\n306 |     if isinstance(index, ABCPeriodIndex):\n307 | \n308 |         majlocator = TimeSeries_DateLocator(\n309 |             freq, dynamic_mode=True, minor_locator=False, plot_obj=subplot\n310 |         )\n311 |         minlocator = TimeSeries_DateLocator(\n312 |             freq, dynamic_mode=True, minor_locator=True, plot_obj=subplot\n313 |         )\n314 |         subplot.xaxis.set_major_locator(majlocator)\n315 |         subplot.xaxis.set_minor_locator(minlocator)\n316 | \n317 |         majformatter = TimeSeries_DateFormatter(\n318 |             freq, dynamic_mode=True, minor_locator=False, plot_obj=subplot\n319 |         )\n320 |         minformatter = TimeSeries_DateFormatter(\n321 |             freq, dynamic_mode=True, minor_locator=True, plot_obj=subplot\n322 |         )\n323 |         subplot.xaxis.set_major_formatter(majformatter)\n324 |         subplot.xaxis.set_minor_formatter(minformatter)\n325 | \n326 |         # x and y coord info\n327 |         subplot.format_coord = functools.partial(_format_coord, freq)\n328 | \n329 |     elif isinstance(index, ABCTimedeltaIndex):\n330 |         subplot.xaxis.set_major_formatter(TimeSeries_TimedeltaFormatter())\n331 |     else:\n332 |         raise TypeError(\"index type not supported\")\n333 | \n334 |     pylab.draw_if_interactive()\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/tools.py:_get_layout`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 86 | def _get_layout(nplots: int, layout=None, layout_type: str = \"box\") -> tuple[int, int]:\n 87 |     if layout is not None:\n 88 |         if not isinstance(layout, (tuple, list)) or len(layout) != 2:\n 89 |             raise ValueError(\"Layout must be a tuple of (rows, columns)\")\n 90 | \n 91 |         nrows, ncols = layout\n 92 | \n 93 |         if nrows == -1 and ncols > 0:\n 94 |             layout = nrows, ncols = (ceil(nplots / ncols), ncols)\n 95 |         elif ncols == -1 and nrows > 0:\n 96 |             layout = nrows, ncols = (nrows, ceil(nplots / nrows))\n 97 |         elif ncols <= 0 and nrows <= 0:\n 98 |             msg = \"At least one dimension of layout must be positive\"\n 99 |             raise ValueError(msg)\n100 | \n101 |         if nrows * ncols < nplots:\n102 |             raise ValueError(\n103 |                 f\"Layout of {nrows}x{ncols} must be larger than required size {nplots}\"\n104 |             )\n105 | \n106 |         return layout\n107 | \n108 |     if layout_type == \"single\":\n109 |         return (1, 1)\n110 |     elif layout_type == \"horizontal\":\n111 |         return (1, nplots)\n112 |     elif layout_type == \"vertical\":\n113 |         return (nplots, 1)\n114 | \n115 |     layouts = {1: (1, 1), 2: (1, 2), 3: (2, 2), 4: (2, 2)}\n116 |     try:\n117 |         return layouts[nplots]\n118 |     except KeyError:\n119 |         k = 1\n120 |         while k**2 < nplots:\n121 |             k += 1\n122 | \n123 |         if (k - 1) * k >= nplots:\n124 |             return k, (k - 1)\n125 |         else:\n126 |             return k, k\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"subplots\", \"DataFrame.plot subplots\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `pandas/plotting/_core.py:boxplot_frame_groupby`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n526 | def boxplot_frame_groupby(\n527 |     grouped,\n528 |     subplots=True,\n529 |     column=None,\n530 |     fontsize=None,\n531 |     rot=0,\n532 |     grid=True,\n533 |     ax=None,\n534 |     figsize=None,\n535 |     layout=None,\n536 |     sharex=False,\n537 |     sharey=True,\n538 |     backend=None,\n539 |     **kwargs,\n540 | ):\n541 |     \"\"\"\n542 |     Make box plots from DataFrameGroupBy data.\n543 | \n544 |     Parameters\n545 |     ----------\n546 |     grouped : Grouped DataFrame\n547 |     subplots : bool\n548 |         * ``False`` - no subplots will be used\n549 |         * ``True`` - create a subplot for each group.\n550 | \n551 |     column : column name or list of names, or vector\n552 |         Can be any valid input to groupby.\n553 |     fontsize : int or str\n554 |     rot : label rotation angle\n555 |     grid : Setting this to True will show the grid\n556 |     ax : Matplotlib axis object, default None\n557 |     figsize : A tuple (width, height) in inches\n558 |     layout : tuple (optional)\n559 |         The layout of the plot: (rows, columns).\n560 |     sharex : bool, default False\n561 |         Whether x-axes will be shared among subplots.\n562 |     sharey : bool, default True\n563 |         Whether y-axes will be shared among subplots.\n564 |     backend : str, default None\n565 |         Backend to use instead of the backend specified in the option\n566 |         ``plotting.backend``. For instance, 'matplotlib'. Alternatively, to\n567 |         specify the ``plotting.backend`` for the whole session, set\n568 |         ``pd.options.plotting.backend``.\n569 | \n570 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0.0\n571 | \n572 |     **kwargs\n573 |         All other plotting keyword arguments to be passed to\n574 |         matplotlib's boxplot function.\n575 | \n576 |     Returns\n577 |     -------\n578 |     dict of key/value = group key/DataFrame.boxplot return value\n579 |     or DataFrame.boxplot return value in case subplots=figures=False\n580 | \n581 |     Examples\n582 |     --------\n583 |     You can create boxplots for grouped data and show them as separate subplots:\n584 | \n585 |     .. plot::\n586 |         :context: close-figs\n587 | \n588 |         >>> import itertools\n589 |         >>> tuples = [t for t in itertools.product(range(1000), range(4))]\n590 |         >>> index = pd.MultiIndex.from_tuples(tuples, names=['lvl0', 'lvl1'])\n591 |         >>> data = np.random.randn(len(index),4)\n592 |         >>> df = pd.DataFrame(data, columns=list('ABCD'), index=index)\n593 |         >>> grouped = df.groupby(level='lvl1')\n594 |         >>> grouped.boxplot(rot=45, fontsize=12, figsize=(8,10))  # doctest: +SKIP\n595 | \n596 |     The ``subplots=False`` option shows the boxplots in a single figure.\n597 | \n598 |     .. plot::\n599 |         :context: close-figs\n600 | \n601 |         >>> grouped.boxplot(subplots=False, rot=45, fontsize=12)  # doctest: +SKIP\n602 |     \"\"\"\n603 |     plot_backend = _get_plot_backend(backend)\n604 |     return plot_backend.boxplot_frame_groupby(\n605 |         grouped,\n606 |         subplots=subplots,\n607 |         column=column,\n608 |         fontsize=fontsize,\n609 |         rot=rot,\n610 |         grid=grid,\n611 |         ax=ax,\n612 |         figsize=figsize,\n613 |         layout=layout,\n614 |         sharex=sharex,\n615 |         sharey=sharey,\n616 |         **kwargs,\n617 |     )\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"DataFrame.plot subplots\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound class `pandas/plotting/_core.py:PlotAccessor`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\nJust show the structure of this class due to response length limitations:\n```\nclass PlotAccessor(PandasObject):\n\n    _common_kinds = (\"line\", \"bar\", \"barh\", \"kde\", \"density\", \"area\", \"hist\", \"box\")\n    _series_kinds = (\"pie\",)\n    _dataframe_kinds = (\"scatter\", \"hexbin\")\n    _kind_aliases = {\"density\": \"kde\"}\n    _all_kinds = _common_kinds + _series_kinds + _dataframe_kinds\n\n    def __init__(self, data) -> None:\n...\n    @staticmethod\n    def _get_call_args(backend_name, data, args, kwargs):\n...\n    def __call__(self, *args, **kwargs):\n...\n    __call__.__doc__ = __doc__\n\n    @Appender(\n        \"\"\"\n        See Also\n        --------\n        matplotlib.pyplot.plot : Plot y versus x as lines and/or markers.\n\n        Examples\n        --------\n\n        .. plot::\n            :context: close-figs\n\n            >>> s = pd.Series([1, 3, 2])\n            >>> s.plot.line()\n            <AxesSubplot:ylabel='Density'>\n\n        .. plot::\n            :context: close-figs\n\n            The following example shows the populations for some animals\n            over the years.\n\n            >>> df = pd.DataFrame({\n            ...    'pig': [20, 18, 489, 675, 1776],\n            ...    'horse': [4, 25, 281, 600, 1900]\n            ...    }, index=[1990, 1997, 2003, 2009, 2014])\n            >>> lines = df.plot.line()\n\n        .. plot::\n           :context: close-figs\n\n           An example with subplots, so an array of axes is returned.\n\n           >>> axes = df.plot.line(subplots=True)\n           >>> type(axes)\n           <class 'numpy.ndarray'>\n\n        .. plot::\n           :context: close-figs\n\n           Let's repeat the same example, but specifying colors for\n           each column (in this case, for each animal).\n\n           >>> axes = df.plot.line(\n           ...     subplots=True, color={\"pig\": \"pink\", \"horse\": \"#742802\"}\n           ... )\n\n        .. plot::\n            :context: close-figs\n\n            The following example shows the relationship between both\n            populations.\n\n            >>> lines = df.plot.line(x='pig', y='horse')\n        \"\"\"\n    )\n    @Substitution(kind=\"line\")\n    @Appender(_bar_or_line_doc)\n    def line(self, x=None, y=None, **kwargs):\n...\n    @Appender(\n        \"\"\"\n        See Also\n        --------\n        DataFrame.plot.barh : Horizontal bar plot.\n        DataFrame.plot : Make plots of a DataFrame.\n        matplotlib.pyplot.bar : Make a bar plot with matplotlib.\n\n        Examples\n        --------\n        Basic plot.\n\n        .. plot::\n            :context: close-figs\n\n            >>> df = pd.DataFrame({'lab':['A', 'B', 'C'], 'val':[10, 30, 20]})\n            >>> ax = df.plot.bar(x='lab', y='val', rot=0)\n\n        Plot a whole dataframe to a bar plot. Each column is assigned a\n        distinct color, and each row is nested in a group along the\n        horizontal axis.\n\n        .. plot::\n            :context: close-figs\n\n            >>> speed = [0.1, 17.5, 40, 48, 52, 69, 88]\n            >>> lifespan = [2, 8, 70, 1.5, 25, 12, 28]\n            >>> index = ['snail', 'pig', 'elephant',\n            ...          'rabbit', 'giraffe', 'coyote', 'horse']\n            >>> df = pd.DataFrame({'speed': speed,\n            ...                    'lifespan': lifespan}, index=index)\n            >>> ax = df.plot.bar(rot=0)\n\n        Plot stacked bar charts for the DataFrame\n\n        .. plot::\n            :context: close-figs\n\n            >>> ax = df.plot.bar(stacked=True)\n\n        Instead of nesting, the figure can be split by column with\n        ``subplots=True``. In this case, a :class:`numpy.ndarray` of\n        :class:`matplotlib.axes.Axes` are returned.\n\n        .. plot::\n            :context: close-figs\n\n            >>> axes = df.plot.bar(rot=0, subplots=True)\n            >>> axes[1].legend(loc=2)  # doctest: +SKIP\n\n        If you don't like the default colours, you can specify how you'd\n        like each column to be colored.\n\n        .. plot::\n            :context: close-figs\n\n            >>> axes = df.plot.bar(\n            ...     rot=0, subplots=True, color={\"speed\": \"red\", \"lifespan\": \"green\"}\n            ... )\n            >>> axes[1].legend(loc=2)  # doctest: +SKIP\n\n        Plot a single column.\n\n        .. plot::\n            :context: close-figs\n\n            >>> ax = df.plot.bar(y='speed', rot=0)\n\n        Plot only selected categories for the DataFrame.\n\n        .. plot::\n            :context: close-figs\n\n            >>> ax = df.plot.bar(x='lifespan', rot=0)\n    \"\"\"\n    )\n    @Substitution(kind=\"bar\")\n    @Appender(_bar_or_line_doc)\n    def bar(self, x=None, y=None, **kwargs):\n...\n    @Appender(\n        \"\"\"\n        See Also\n        --------\n        DataFrame.plot.bar: Vertical bar plot.\n        DataFrame.plot : Make plots of DataFrame using matplotlib.\n        matplotlib.axes.Axes.bar : Plot a vertical bar plot using matplotlib.\n\n        Examples\n        --------\n        Basic example\n\n        .. plot::\n            :context: close-figs\n\n            >>> df = pd.DataFrame({'lab': ['A', 'B', 'C'], 'val': [10, 30, 20]})\n            >>> ax = df.plot.barh(x='lab', y='val')\n\n        Plot a